; Reverse zone for 192.168.1.0/24 — address to name, the opposite direction to the forward zone.
;
; This is what `dig -x 192.168.1.20` and `ping -a` use, and what a mail server checks when it
; decides whether to trust a connection. A forward record with no matching PTR is a very common
; misconfiguration, which is why this zone ships beside example.com.
;
; The zone NAME is the network's first three octets REVERSED, then .in-addr.arpa:
;     192.168.1.0/24   ->   1.168.192.in-addr.arpa
;     10.0.1.0/24      ->   1.0.10.in-addr.arpa
; If you change the addressing, change the zone name in /etc/bind/named.conf to match.

$TTL 60

@       IN  SOA     ns1.example.com. admin.example.com. (
                    2026081301  ; Serial  <-- increase this on EVERY edit
                    3600        ; Refresh
                    600         ; Retry
                    604800      ; Expire
                    60 )        ; Negative cache TTL

@       IN  NS      ns1.example.com.

; PTR — the last octet of the address, mapped back to a full name. The trailing dot is not
; optional here: without it BIND appends the zone name and you get a nonsense answer like
; www.example.com.1.168.192.in-addr.arpa.
;
; Keep these in step with example.com.zone. They are a separate zone and do NOT update
; themselves when you add an A record.
10      IN  PTR     ns1.example.com.
20      IN  PTR     www.example.com.
30      IN  PTR     mail.example.com.
101     IN  PTR     host1.example.com.
102     IN  PTR     host2.example.com.
